WEBVTT

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.660
A meeting ends.

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:03.400
What should change next time?

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:06.830
Northstar Studio asks its five teammates for feedback on

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.440
their quarterly meeting.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.970
A short survey gives each person a way to

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.560
answer before the group moves on.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.020
Here is how the host turns those answers into

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.200
a useful record.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.380
Maya creates a survey with five questions.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.040
She asks for a rating, a useful part of

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.030
the meeting, clearer next steps, a ranked priority, and

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:28.940
one practical change.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.600
She opens it and shares the session code.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.430
People join on their own devices with no account

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:35.531
to create.

00:00:35.621 --> 00:00:38.341
Priya gives the meeting five out of five.

00:00:38.421 --> 00:00:41.621
She chooses the customer discussion as the most useful

00:00:41.621 --> 00:00:42.281
part.

00:00:42.361 --> 00:00:44.981
Each answer saves as she goes.

00:00:45.061 --> 00:00:47.091
This survey is anonymous.

00:00:47.221 --> 00:00:50.611
The host sees the totals and written feedback without

00:00:50.611 --> 00:00:52.598
names attached to those answers.

00:00:52.658 --> 00:00:55.658
The questions can ask for more than a score.

00:00:55.658 --> 00:00:59.298
Priya ranks customer evidence first for next quarter, then

00:00:59.298 --> 00:01:01.308
adds a written suggestion.

00:01:01.538 --> 00:01:03.938
Another teammate says the next steps are not clear

00:01:04.508 --> 00:01:06.288
yet and explains why.

00:01:06.338 --> 00:01:09.288
Who owns the first prototype and when will the

00:01:09.288 --> 00:01:10.134
team review it?

00:01:10.224 --> 00:01:12.824
Priya checks her answers and sends them.

00:01:12.904 --> 00:01:15.304
She can still make a change until the host

00:01:15.304 --> 00:01:16.544
closes the survey.

00:01:16.864 --> 00:01:19.824
Back on the main screen, Maya sees five out

00:01:19.854 --> 00:01:21.464
of five finished.

00:01:21.544 --> 00:01:24.424
She closes the survey so the answers are fixed

00:01:24.504 --> 00:01:26.634
and the team can read the results together.

00:01:26.749 --> 00:01:29.299
The result visuals make the pattern clear.

00:01:29.349 --> 00:01:32.059
The average rating is four out of five.

00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:35.299
Customer discussion was the most picked part, but only

00:01:35.379 --> 00:01:38.099
three people say the next steps are clear.

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:41.279
One says not yet, and one is unsure.

00:01:41.329 --> 00:01:44.139
A good overall rating still leaves a problem to

00:01:44.139 --> 00:01:44.630
solve.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.820
The ranked results put customer evidence first.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:52.020
Written feedback asks for an earlier brief, shorter updates,

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:55.520
more time to talk, and clear owners and dates.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.610
These are concrete changes the host can use when

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.300
planning the next meeting.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.550
Read the words as well as the charts.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.000
Maya opens the session report.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:08.050
The results and written responses stay together in one

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:08.620
place.

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:11.200
She saves it for one year and downloads a

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.030
copy.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.930
Use a survey after a meeting, a workshop, or

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:16.590
a training session.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.730
Ask what helped, what was unclear, and what should

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:20.385
happen next.
